# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# This is configuration, not page code: Pages reads it from the root of the
# published directory, applies the rules, and never serves the file itself.
#
# The panel had no headers of its own and inherited only Cloudflare's defaults.
# Every rule below was checked against what the page actually loads:
#
#   ./styles.css, which imports ./design-tokens.css   same origin
#   ./app.js, a module, the only script                same origin
#   ./data/stats.json, read with fetch()               same origin
#   fonts.googleapis.com, one stylesheet               third party
#   fonts.gstatic.com, the font files it pulls         third party
#   image.tmdb.org, cast and crew photographs          third party
#   letterboxd.com, ~200 outbound links                navigation only, not loaded
#
# There are no inline scripts, no inline event handlers and no
# other origin anywhere in the page. s.ltrbxd.com appears once in
# design-tokens.css as a comment recording where the palette was read from;
# nothing fetches it, so it is deliberately not allowed here.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' https://image.tmdb.org; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000

# Why each one is here.
#
# Content-Security-Policy
#   script-src 'self' is the rule that matters. The page builds every element
#   with textContent and createTextNode and holds no innerHTML, so a hostile
#   film title from TMDB is already inert text. This is what that protection
#   falls back on the day somebody adds one innerHTML to a renderer, or the day
#   the Google Fonts origin serves something it should not. Note the omission:
#   'unsafe-inline' is NOT in script-src, and adding it would give up the whole
#   point. Keep scripts in app.js. An inline <script> in index.html will not run
#   under this policy.
#
#   'unsafe-inline' IS in style-src, on purpose. Inline CSS on this page buys an
#   attacker defacement at most: there is no form, no session, no cookie and no
#   private value in the DOM, and a compromise of the Google Fonts origin would
#   supply arbitrary CSS anyway. Meanwhile a strict style-src silently strips a
#   page that has no build step to catch it, on a Monday, unattended. The trade
#   is not worth it in that direction. (The word-cloud sizing in app.js assigns
#   through element.style, which CSP does not govern either way.)
#
#   default-src 'none' means anything not listed is refused rather than
#   inherited, so a new kind of subresource has to be added here deliberately.
#
# frame-ancestors 'none' and X-Frame-Options: DENY
#   The live site could be framed by any origin, which makes it usable as
#   borrowed credibility inside someone else's page. frame-ancestors is the rule
#   modern browsers honour; X-Frame-Options is the older one and is kept for the
#   browsers that still only read that. frame-ancestors is also the reason this
#   is a headers file and not a <meta> tag in index.html: delivered through a
#   meta tag, that directive is ignored and the framing stays possible.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options and Referrer-Policy
#   Cloudflare already sends both. They are restated so that adding this file
#   cannot quietly drop them.
#
# Permissions-Policy
#   The panel asks for none of these. This denies them ahead of time, so a
#   script that should not be running cannot prompt the reader for a location or
#   a camera in this page's name.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security
#   Redundant today and kept anyway: the whole .dev top-level domain is on the
#   HSTS preload list, so browsers already refuse plaintext to pages.dev. It
#   starts doing real work the day this panel moves to a custom domain on
#   another suffix. No includeSubDomains and no preload, because neither is this
#   site's to assert.
